19
00:01:16.251 --> 00:01:24.507
The two disagree about significance precisely when the discordant count is small, so prefer the exact test, which costs nothing to compute.

20
00:01:24.787 --> 00:01:27.453
There’s also an external argument for this test.

21
00:01:27.733 --> 00:01:46.272
Dietterich (1998) compared approximate tests for deciding whether one learning algorithm beats another and found McNemar’s test had low Type I error (it rarely declares a difference that isn’t there), making it the recommended choice when each system can be run only once on a fixed test set.

22
00:01:46.552 --> 00:01:49.688
That’s the usual situation for a prompt comparison.

23
00:01:49.968 --> 00:01:55.771
Report the paired difference with its interval as your main result, and the McNemar p-value as a check.
